<section id="conclusion" class="section">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2>07 Conclusion And Insights: where shall we head to?</h2>
    <div class="report-prose conclusion-prose">
      <p>
        The evaluation places GPT-6-Astra’s embodied capability frontier between{' '}
        <strong>flexible task interpretation and reliable physical execution</strong>. With one
        annotated demonstration per task, it ranks second among eight models and leads on short-horizon tasks, while trailing on tabletop and long-horizon tasks. The recordings show
        how it explores alternative approaches, recovers disrupted goals and uses an unsuccessful
        attempt to revise its next action.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Precise alignment and manipulation remain clear execution bottlenecks</strong>, with
        leading specialized policies retaining a substantial advantage on precision-sensitive tasks.
        The logs also expose a control challenge: the agent selects corrections between action
        chunks, while contact and grasp stability can change within a chunk. Large EEF corrections
        and awkward object poses raise safety concerns for physical deployment. These findings
        motivate four research directions.
      </p>
      <h3>Combine specialized policies with agents</h3>
      <p>
        The comparisons show{' '}
        <strong>
          complementary capabilities in frontier agents and low-level control policies
        </strong>
        . GPT-6-Astra’s task interpretation and recovery offer ways to handle unfamiliar states that
        challenge demonstration-trained policies. Specialized policies provide responsive, precise
        execution within the conditions they have learned. A combined system could assign
        exploration and goal revision to the agent, while a local controller maintains the fast
        feedback needed for contact-sensitive operations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Coupling these capabilities requires an explicit handoff.</strong> The agent can
        specify goals and contact assumptions. The controller can report grasp loss or unexpected
        contact, interrupt execution and return control to the agent.{' '}
        <a
          class="source-link"
          href="https://rpent.readthedocs.io/en/latest/rst_source/development/architecture.html"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          RPent
        </a>{' '}
        <a class="paper-citation" href="#ref-rpent" role="doc-biblioref" aria-label="Reference 9">
          [9]
        </a>
        , which exposes VLA skills as retryable tools to a planner, provides one architecture for
        coupling a planner to learned skills. Its performance under scene changes and
        contact-sensitive tasks is a useful direction for evaluation.
      </p>
      <h3>Teach smaller policies to plan, explore, and recover</h3>
      <p>
        A second direction is to{' '}
        <strong>transfer the agent’s action strategy to smaller on-device policies</strong>. Its
        rollouts include attempts to obtain better observations and corrections after failed
        actions. Training examples could connect the{' '}
        <strong>goal, failure state, revised strategy and observed outcome</strong>, retaining
        unsuccessful corrections as well as successful recovery. A policy could learn when to
        inspect or retry, and when to abandon an ineffective procedure.
      </p>
      <p>
        This broadens behavior cloning toward <strong>intention imitation</strong>: learning which
        corrective strategy fits a goal and observed state. The challenge is to preserve that
        relationship across scenes and embodiments without copying the teacher’s coordinates or
        mistakes. Agent-generated exploration could make data collection more scalable than human
        demonstrations alone, provided transfer quality, safety and collection cost hold up.
      </p>
      <h3>Turn exploratory experience into transferable knowledge and tools</h3>
      <p>
        The apple recovery is consistent with using feedback to revise the next attempt. Turning such
        experience into reusable contact checks, recovery procedures or tools could improve both the
        agent and its harness. This offers a route to studying{' '}
        <strong>recursive self-improvement (RSI)</strong> in embodied systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        The test is whether a lesson improves execution on held-out tasks. An exploration-derived
        tool could be evaluated for fewer retries or higher completion, including with a different
        base model. Its applicable conditions and completion checks should be recorded before it is
        frozen and compared on new tasks against raw-history and no-memory alternatives.
      </p>
      <h3>Make safety part of the action policy</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Task success alone is insufficient for physical deployment.</strong> An object can
        be recovered after a drop, but the drop may already have caused damage. Large corrections,
        obstructed closures and uncertain contact heights require constraints during execution.
        These include motion and force limits, grasp and clearance checks, coordinated bimanual
        control, and the ability to interrupt a hazardous action. The agent also needs to recognize
        uncertain geometry and seek a better observation or assistance.
      </p>
      <p>
        For embodied research, these results motivate treating{' '}
        <strong>
          task understanding, exploration and recovery as capabilities to couple with precise
          control
        </strong>
        . The central question is how to preserve the agent’s flexibility while making its actions
        accurate, timely and safe—and how to transfer useful experience to later tasks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
